  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1203 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSaturday into Saturday night, an upper level trough will move \ninto and through the southern plains. Although there will be a \ndryline moving into the area late in the day, models, include CAMs\nthat go out that far, have greatly reduced precipitation chances \n(20% or less) for west-central Texas. A few showers may develop, \nmainly north of I-10, but rainfall amounts are expected to be \nlight. We will see one more day of highs in the upper 60s to lower\n70s on Saturday as well. \n\nA cold front will move through by Sunday morning, knocking highs \ndown 10 to 15 degrees, resulting in temperatures only warming into\nthe mid 50s to lower 60s. North winds will persist into Monday, \nresulting in continued cooler than normal highs through the middle\nof next week. Medium range models diverge in their solutions from\nSunday night through the rest of the forecast, and also have very\nlittle run to run consistency. There had previously been a \nsignificant upper level low moving into the area around \nThanksgiving, but this low has been replaced by ridging in the new\nrun from that model solution. Thus, confidence is low that we \nwill see significant precipitation through the next week, and \ntemperatures should remain near or a few degrees below normal.",
